# Message catalogues

A catalogue holds the messages for one locale. It is a JSON file named by
the locale's tag, such as `locales/de.json`. Messages are written in
[ICU MessageFormat](https://unicode-org.github.io/icu/userguide/format_parse/messages/),
which translation tools such as Crowdin, Lokalise, Phrase, Weblate and
Tolgee understand.

```json
{
  "@@locale": "de",
  "cart.items": "{count, plural, =0 {Ihr Warenkorb ist leer} one {# Artikel} other {# Artikel}}",
  "nav": {
    "home": "Startseite"
  }
}
```

Nested objects are flattened with dots, so the last message's key is
`nav.home`. Keys that start with `@` are notes for people and tools, as in
the ARB format. They are not messages:

- `@@locale` names the catalogue's locale and must match its file name.
- `@@x-review` marks a catalogue that still needs checking by a native
  speaker. `check` lists those catalogues.
- `@key` objects, such as `{"description": "..."}`, describe a message for
  translators.

## The source catalogue

One locale is the source, `en` unless you pass `--source`. Its catalogue
decides which messages exist and what arguments they take.

The other catalogues translate it. A message missing from a translation
falls back to the next locale the translator would try, and finally to the
source. A missing translation never shows a blank or a key.

## Writing messages

```text
Hello, {name}!
{count, plural, =0 {No items} one {# item} other {# items}}
{gender, select, female {She} male {He} other {They}} replied.
You came {place, selectordinal, one {#st} two {#nd} few {#rd} other {#th}}.
Saved on {when, date, long} at {at, time, short}.
{ratio, number, percent} done.
{guests, plural, offset:1 =0 {Nobody} =1 {{host}} other {{host} and # others}} came.
```

- `{name}` is text.
- `{n, plural, ...}` chooses a form by the number's plural category:
  `zero`, `one`, `two`, `few`, `many` and `other`. Each language uses only
  some of them: English uses `one` and `other`, and Polish adds `few` and
  `many`. `=0` matches an exact number. Inside a plural, `#` is the number,
  written for the locale.
- `{n, selectordinal, ...}` does the same for positions: 1st, 2nd, 3rd.
- `{x, select, ...}` chooses by a string, with `other` for anything else.
- `{n, number}` writes a whole number with the locale's grouping, and
  `{r, number, percent}` writes a fraction as a percentage.
- `{d, date, style}` and `{t, time, style}` write dates and times. The
  styles are `short`, `medium`, `long` and `full`.
- An apostrophe quotes syntax: `'{name}'` is the literal text `{name}`, and
  `''` is an apostrophe. An apostrophe anywhere else is itself, so "It's"
  needs no escaping.

Each argument gets a Gleam type from how the messages use it: `String`
for text and selects, `Int` for numbers and plurals, `Float` for
percentages, `calendar.Date` for dates and `calendar.TimeOfDay` for times.
Every translation must agree with the source.

Plural counts are whole numbers.

## Generating the module

```sh
gleam run -m howdy/i18n/cli -- gen
```

This reads `locales/` and writes `src/<app>/messages.gleam`, with a function
for each message. The key `cart.items`, or `cartItems`, becomes the
function `cart_items`:

```gleam
/// cart.items: {count, plural, =0 {Your cart is empty} one {# item} other {# items}}
pub fn cart_items(translator: Translator, count count: Int) -> String
```

The module also exports `locales`, the catalogues there are, which is handy
for building a negotiator:
`locale.negotiator(messages.locales, default: messages.source)`.

The translations are compiled in, so nothing is read from disk at run time.
Run `gen` again after changing a catalogue, and commit the module with the
catalogues.

The options are:

- `--locales=<dir>`: where the catalogues are.
- `--out=<file>`: the module to write.
- `--source=<locale>`: the source locale.
- `--standalone`: write a module that needs nothing but the standard
  library. Its functions take the catalogue's locale as a string instead
  of a translator. Only text, whole numbers and cardinal plurals are
  allowed. This is how Howdy's core ships its validation messages without
  depending on `howdy_i18n`.

### While you work

With [hot reload](/docs/cli/overview), regenerate the module whenever a
catalogue changes. Do this in your `dev/<app>_dev.gleam`:

```gleam
import gleam/erlang/process
import howdy/dev
import howdy/i18n/cli
import my_app

pub fn main() {
  let assert Ok(_) =
    dev.new(my_app.app)
    |> dev.watch("locales")
    |> dev.before_build(fn(_changed) { cli.execute(["gen"]) })
    |> dev.run
  process.sleep_forever()
}
```

`gen` leaves the module alone when nothing in it changed. When a catalogue
has an error, it is shown over the page, as a compiler error would be.

## Checking

```sh
gleam run -m howdy/i18n/cli -- check
```

`check` reports problems without writing anything. When there are errors
it exits with a failure, so you can run it in CI.

- Errors stop `gen`. They are invalid ICU syntax, an argument the source
  message lacks, an argument used as two different types, or two keys that
  would become the same function.
- Warnings fall back and do not stop it. They are a message missing from a
  translation, a message the source lacks, or a plural form the language
  needs but the message does not give.
- Notes list the catalogues marked `@@x-review`.

## Adding a locale

```sh
gleam run -m howdy/i18n/cli -- add pt-BR
```

This copies the source catalogue to `locales/pt-BR.json` and marks it
`"@@x-review": "untranslated"` for a translator to work through. Remove the
mark once it is done.
